WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.487
IN and JOIN may appear to answer similar questions, but they have different semantics.

00:00:05.487 --> 00:00:09.352
IN asks whether an outer value belongs to a set.

00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:12.677
A join returns matching row combinations.

00:00:12.677 --> 00:00:17.587
If one patient has multiple admissions, the join can repeat that patient.

00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:21.037
The IN query keeps the patient-level outer grain.

00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:24.817
Run both queries and compare the outputs carefully.

00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:30.652
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product.

00:00:30.652 --> 00:00:36.337
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.337 --> 00:00:44.237
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:44.237 --> 00:00:49.812
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:49.812 --> 00:00:55.721
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
